## The problem
If any code can reach into an object and change any field, every invariant you care about (balance never negative, email always lowercase, state machine only moves forward) is enforced by convention only. One careless caller breaks it, and you need to search the whole codebase to find who did. Encapsulation means: bundle data with the behaviour that operates on it, and hide the representation behind a small public surface so the object can protect its own invariants.

## Mechanisms in TypeScript/JavaScript
- `private` / `protected` / `readonly` keywords: compile-time only. They vanish after transpilation, so plain JS or a type cast (`(acct as any).balance`) bypasses them.
- ECMAScript private fields `#balance`: enforced at runtime by the engine. Not reachable via `obj["#balance"]`, `Object.keys`, or JSON.stringify. Not accessible in subclasses either.
- Closures: a factory function that keeps state in local variables and returns only methods. This is the pre-class way to get true privacy.
- Module scope: anything not exported is private to the file; often the simplest form of encapsulation in Node.

```ts
class Account {
  #balance = 0;                       // runtime-private
  constructor(readonly id: string) {}
  deposit(n: number) {
    if (n <= 0) throw new RangeError("amount must be positive");
    this.#balance += n;
  }
  withdraw(n: number) {
    if (n > this.#balance) throw new Error("insufficient funds");
    this.#balance -= n;
  }
  get balance() { return this.#balance; }   // read-only view
}

// closure version
function makeCounter() {
  let n = 0;
  return { inc: () => ++n, value: () => n };
}
```

## Why it matters beyond hiding
- Invariants live in one place: validation sits in `deposit`, not in 20 callers.
- You can change the representation (store cents as integer, cache a sum) without touching callers. This is the real payoff: a stable interface over a changeable implementation.
- Prefer behaviour over getters/setters. A class with a getter and setter for every field is just a struct with extra typing; "Tell, don't ask" is the guideline.

## Pitfalls and follow-ups
- Returning a reference to an internal array or object leaks the state: `get items() { return this.#items; }` lets callers mutate it. Return a copy or a readonly view.
- `private` in TS does not exist at runtime, so never rely on it for security.
- Per-instance closures cost memory (each object gets its own function copies), while class methods live on the prototype once. Matters for millions of objects.
- Java/C++: access modifiers `private/protected/public` are enforced by the compiler and (in Java) the JVM; C++ also has `friend`, which deliberately breaks encapsulation for a named class or function. Java default (package-private) access has no equivalent keyword in TS.

