## Principle
Software entities should be open for extension but closed for modification: add new behaviour by adding new code, not by editing code that already works and is tested.

## Violation
```ts
function discount(type: string, amt: number) {
  if (type === "regular") return amt * 0.0;
  if (type === "vip") return amt * 0.1;
  if (type === "black-friday") return amt * 0.3;   // every new type edits this function
  throw new Error("unknown");
}
```
Each new rule modifies shared code and risks regressions in the others.

## Fix: depend on an abstraction, add implementations
```ts
interface DiscountPolicy { apply(amount: number): number }
class Vip implements DiscountPolicy { apply(a: number) { return a * 0.9; } }
class BlackFriday implements DiscountPolicy { apply(a: number) { return a * 0.7; } }

function checkout(amount: number, policy: DiscountPolicy) { return policy.apply(amount); }
```
Adding "Student" is a new class; `checkout` is untouched. A registry (`Map<string, DiscountPolicy>`) replaces the switch for dynamic lookup.

## Ways to get OCP
Strategy, Decorator, Template Method, plugin registries, middleware chains, higher-order functions (pass a function). In functional style, passing a callback is OCP without classes.

## Caveats
- You can't close a module against every future change. Pick the axes of change you actually see (you have seen two variations already). The "rule of three" is a good trigger: tolerate one or two if/else branches, abstract on the third.
- Premature abstraction costs readability; a simple `switch` is fine for stable, closed sets (days of week).
- Exhaustive `switch` on a TS discriminated union with a `never` check is a pragmatic alternative: you modify the switch, but the compiler lists every place to update.

## Interview follow-up
"Isn't adding a class modification too?" Existing, tested code stays untouched; wiring in one composition root changes. That is acceptable.

