## Principle
Clients should not be forced to depend on methods they do not use. Prefer several small, role-specific interfaces over one fat one.

## Violation
```ts
interface Worker {
  work(): void;
  eat(): void;
  sleep(): void;
}
class Robot implements Worker {
  work() {}
  eat() { throw new Error("robots do not eat"); }     // forced, and an LSP smell too
  sleep() { throw new Error("n/a"); }
}
```

## Fix
```ts
interface Workable { work(): void }
interface Feedable { eat(): void }
interface Restable { sleep(): void }

class Human implements Workable, Feedable, Restable { work(){} eat(){} sleep(){} }
class Robot implements Workable { work() {} }

function runShift(w: Workable) { w.work(); }      // asks only for what it needs
```

## Why it matters
- Fewer reasons to recompile or retest clients when an unrelated method changes.
- Easier mocks: a service needing only `get(id)` should take `Pick<Repo, "get">` or a small interface and be tested with a one-line fake.
- Clean separation of read vs write models (CQRS-like `ReadRepo` and `WriteRepo`).

```ts
type ReadOnlyUsers = Pick<UserRepo, "findById" | "list">;
function showProfile(users: ReadOnlyUsers, id: string) { /* cannot call delete */ }
```

## Pitfalls
- Splitting too far: dozens of one-method interfaces become noise. Group by client role.
- A Node/TS idiom: define the interface where it is consumed (client side), a Go-style approach, so the consumer states exactly what it needs.
- Java/C++: classes implementing a fat interface end up with empty or throwing methods. In Java, default methods can soften this but are not a design fix. A C++ class inheriting a fat abstract base has the same problem.

## Related
ISP is SRP for interfaces, and it supports LSP (nobody has to fake a method) and DIP (small abstractions are easier to depend on).

solid:4.body:
## Principle
- High-level modules (business rules) should not depend on low-level modules (DB, HTTP, SMTP). Both should depend on abstractions.
- Abstractions should not depend on details; details depend on abstractions.
- DIP is a design principle. Dependency Injection (DI) is a technique that supplies dependencies from outside. Inversion of Control is the broader idea of the framework calling you. They are related, not synonyms.

## Violation
```ts
import { Pool } from "pg";
class SignupService {
  private db = new Pool({ connectionString: process.env.DB });   // hard-wired detail
  async signup(email: string) { await this.db.query("INSERT ..."); }
}
```
Cannot test without Postgres; cannot change the store without editing business code.

## Fix: invert and inject
```ts
interface UserRepo { create(email: string): Promise<void> }
interface Mailer { welcome(email: string): Promise<void> }

class SignupService {
  constructor(private repo: UserRepo, private mailer: Mailer) {}   // constructor injection
  async signup(email: string) { await this.repo.create(email); await this.mailer.welcome(email); }
}

// composition root: the only place that knows concrete classes
const svc = new SignupService(new PgUserRepo(pool), new SesMailer());

// test
const svc2 = new SignupService({ create: async () => {} }, { welcome: async () => {} });
```
The interface is owned by the high-level module (SignupService's side); PgUserRepo implements it. The source-code dependency arrow points inward, hence "inversion".

## DI in Node
- Plain constructor or function-argument injection plus one composition root (usually enough; no framework).
- Factory functions: `makeSignup({ repo, mailer })` returns a handler (closure-based).
- Frameworks: NestJS (decorators, built-in container), InversifyJS, tsyringe, Awilix. Containers resolve the graph and manage lifetimes (singleton vs per-request).
- Module mocking (`jest.mock`, `vi.mock`) works on the import graph, but it is a workaround, not DI.

## Injection styles
Constructor (preferred: dependencies explicit, object always valid), setter (optional deps), parameter (per-call), service locator (an anti-pattern: hides dependencies).

## Pitfalls
- Container magic hides the object graph and failures arrive at runtime.
- Circular dependencies mean the responsibilities are split wrongly.
- Do not create an interface for everything; invert at boundaries that change or need faking (DB, network, clock, randomness).

patterns-1:1.body:
## Problem
Code that does `new ConcreteClass()` everywhere is tied to that class. When the concrete type depends on config, input or environment (which payment provider, which file parser), creation logic leaks into callers. Factory centralises object creation behind an interface.

## Simple factory / factory function
```ts
interface Notifier { send(to: string, msg: string): Promise<void> }
class EmailNotifier implements Notifier { async send() {} }
class SmsNotifier implements Notifier { async send() {} }

function createNotifier(kind: "email" | "sms"): Notifier {
  switch (kind) {
    case "email": return new EmailNotifier();
    case "sms": return new SmsNotifier();
  }
}
```
(Not a GoF pattern strictly, but the most common form.)

## Factory Method (GoF)
A base class declares a creation method that subclasses override to decide which class to instantiate; the base class's own logic uses the product via the interface.
```ts
abstract class Exporter {
  export(data: unknown[]) { const w = this.createWriter(); return w.write(data); }
  protected abstract createWriter(): Writer;
}
class CsvExporter extends Exporter { protected createWriter() { return new CsvWriter(); } }
```

## Abstract Factory
Creates families of related objects that must be used together, behind one interface.
```ts
interface UiFactory { button(): Button; checkbox(): Checkbox }
class DarkFactory implements UiFactory { /* dark button, dark checkbox */ }
class LightFactory implements UiFactory { /* ... */ }
function render(f: UiFactory) { f.button(); f.checkbox(); }  // consistent family
```
Other examples: a cloud-provider factory returning matching Storage, Queue and Secrets clients; a DB factory returning Connection, Query builder and Migrator for Postgres vs MySQL.

## Comparison
- Simple factory: one function, a switch; adding a type edits it (OCP bend).
- Factory Method: inheritance-based, one product, subclass decides.
- Abstract Factory: composition-based, several related products, swapping the whole family at once.

## When to use / not
Use when type depends on runtime data, when you need to hide constructors, or inject a test fake. Skip it when there is one class and no variation; a plain `new` is fine. In JS, a registry map (`Map<string, () => Notifier>`) replaces the switch and honours OCP.

patterns-1:2.body:
## Problem
Objects with many optional parameters lead to telescoping constructors (`new Req(url, undefined, undefined, 5000, true)`) where callers cannot tell which argument is which, or to half-initialised objects via setters. Builder constructs a complex object step by step and creates it only when ready (and valid).

## Example: fluent builder
```ts
class HttpRequest {
  constructor(readonly url: string, readonly method: string, readonly headers: Readonly<Record<string,string>>,
              readonly body?: string, readonly timeoutMs = 5000) {}
}
class HttpRequestBuilder {
  private method = "GET"; private headers: Record<string,string> = {}; private body?: string; private timeoutMs = 5000;
  constructor(private url: string) {}
  withMethod(m: string) { this.method = m; return this; }
  header(k: string, v: string) { this.headers[k] = v; return this; }
  json(obj: unknown) { this.body = JSON.stringify(obj); return this.header("content-type", "application/json"); }
  timeout(ms: number) { this.timeoutMs = ms; return this; }
  build() {
    if (this.method === "GET" && this.body) throw new Error("GET cannot have a body");   // validate here
    return new HttpRequest(this.url, this.method, { ...this.headers }, this.body, this.timeoutMs);
  }
}
const req = new HttpRequestBuilder("/users").withMethod("POST").json({ name: "a" }).timeout(1000).build();
```

## Benefits
- Readable call sites; optional parameters are named.
- The product can be immutable: only built once, all fields set.
- Centralised validation in `build()`.
- Same builder steps can build different representations (the GoF Director idea).

## JS/TS-native alternatives
- Options object with defaults: `createServer({ port: 80, tls: false })`. Often the better choice in TS: type-checked, no extra class.
- Real-world builders: query builders (Knex `knex("t").where().limit()`), Mongoose queries, Jest/Zod chains, `URLSearchParams`, Java's `StringBuilder` and Lombok `@Builder`.

## Pitfalls
- Reusing a builder after `build()` can share mutable state; copy on build.
- A fluent builder doesn't give compile-time guarantees that required steps happened (use required constructor args or a step-builder with staged interfaces).
- Overkill for objects with 2-3 fields.
- Differs from Factory: Factory picks which class (one call); Builder controls how one complex object is assembled (many calls).

patterns-1:3.body:
## Problem
You have code that expects interface A and a library or legacy class that offers interface B, which you cannot or should not modify. Adapter wraps B and exposes A, translating calls and data. Think of a travel plug adapter.

## Example: swapping payment SDKs
```ts
// what our app wants
interface PaymentGateway { charge(amountCents: number, currency: string): Promise<string> }

// third-party SDK we cannot change
class LegacyPayClient { makePayment(opts: { amount: number; cur: string }): { txn: string } { return { txn: "t1" }; } }

class LegacyPayAdapter implements PaymentGateway {
  constructor(private client: LegacyPayClient) {}
  async charge(amountCents: number, currency: string) {
    const r = this.client.makePayment({ amount: amountCents / 100, cur: currency.toUpperCase() });
    return r.txn;
  }
}
```
The domain code depends only on `PaymentGateway`; replacing the vendor means writing one new adapter.

## Object vs class adapter
- Object adapter (above): composition, holds an instance of the adaptee. Preferred.
- Class adapter: inherits from the adaptee and implements the target (needs multiple inheritance: C++ yes; Java/TS only if the target is an interface).

## Where you see it
- Wrapping a callback API into promises (`util.promisify`).
- Mapping an external API's DTOs to your domain model (anti-corruption layer, often in DDD).
- Database drivers behind a Repository; different cloud SDKs behind one Storage interface.
- Express adapters for serverless handlers (event to req/res).

## Adapter vs related patterns
- Adapter changes an interface to a different one. Decorator keeps the same interface and adds behaviour. Facade defines a new simpler interface over many classes. Proxy keeps the same interface and controls access.

## Pitfalls
- Leaky adaptation: the semantics (errors, retries, units, pagination) must be translated too, not just method names.
- Adapter sprawl; if you own both sides, change the interface instead.
- Unit conversions (cents vs dollars) are classic bug sources; test the adapter at the boundary.

patterns-1:4.body:
## Problem
You want to add responsibilities (logging, caching, retry, auth, compression) to an object without subclassing. Subclassing is static and explodes combinatorially: LoggedCachedRetryingClient, LoggedRetryingClient... A Decorator wraps an object that has the same interface and adds behaviour before or after delegating. Wrappers stack in any order at runtime.

## Example
```ts
interface Http { get(url: string): Promise<string> }
class FetchHttp implements Http { async get(u: string) { return (await fetch(u)).text(); } }

class LoggingHttp implements Http {
  constructor(private inner: Http) {}
  async get(u: string) { const t = Date.now(); try { return await this.inner.get(u); }
    finally { console.log(u, Date.now() - t, "ms"); } }
}
class RetryHttp implements Http {
  constructor(private inner: Http, private tries = 3) {}
  async get(u: string) {
    for (let i = 1; ; i++) { try { return await this.inner.get(u); } catch (e) { if (i >= this.tries) throw e; } }
  }
}
const http: Http = new LoggingHttp(new RetryHttp(new FetchHttp()));   // order matters
```
Logging outside retry logs one line per call; logging inside retry logs every attempt.

## Middleware is decoration of a handler
Express/Koa middleware wraps the downstream handler: each function receives (req, res, next) and chooses to run code before, call `next()`, then run code after. Higher-order functions are the functional form:
```ts
const withTiming = <A extends unknown[], R>(fn: (...a: A) => R) => (...a: A): R => {
  const s = performance.now(); try { return fn(...a); } finally { console.log(performance.now() - s); }
};
```
TS/ES decorators (`@Log()` on methods, as in NestJS) are syntax sugar for the same wrapping, applied at class definition.

## Compared to inheritance and related patterns
- Decorator adds behaviour dynamically; subclass does it statically.
- Proxy usually controls access with the same interface and decides itself when to create/forward; Decorator is composed by the client to add features. Structurally almost identical; intent differs.
- Chain of Responsibility may stop propagation; a decorator always delegates.

## Pitfalls
- Order sensitivity (auth before cache, not after).
- Many small wrapper objects, harder stack traces and debugging.
- Wrapper must forward the whole interface, so keep interfaces small.
- Identity checks (`===`, `instanceof`) fail on the wrapper.

patterns-1:5.body:
## Problem
A task needs several classes in a complex subsystem used in the right order with the right configuration. Every caller then repeats the knowledge and gets coupled to the internals. A Facade provides a single, simple interface for the common use cases, hiding the subsystem.

## Example: placing an order
```ts
class OrderFacade {
  constructor(private inventory: Inventory, private payments: Payments,
              private shipping: Shipping, private notifier: Notifier) {}

  async placeOrder(userId: string, cart: Cart): Promise<OrderId> {
    await this.inventory.reserve(cart.items);
    try {
      const charge = await this.payments.charge(userId, cart.total);
      const id = await this.shipping.schedule(userId, cart.items);
      await this.notifier.confirm(userId, id);
      return id;
    } catch (e) {
      await this.inventory.release(cart.items);   // compensation
      throw e;
    }
  }
}
```
The controller calls `placeOrder`; it doesn't know four subsystems exist.

## Characteristics
- The subsystem is still accessible directly for advanced users (a facade is a convenience, not a wall).
- It does not add new features: it orchestrates existing ones.
- Reduces coupling: clients depend on one class; subsystem can change behind it.

## Real examples
jQuery's `$()`, an SDK's high-level client wrapping HTTP, auth and retries, a service layer over several repositories, an API gateway or BFF fronting microservices (a distributed facade), the `fs/promises` module over low-level file operations.

## Facade vs others
- Adapter: makes an existing interface fit another; Facade defines a new, simpler one for a whole subsystem.
- Mediator: components talk through it and know it; with Facade, the subsystem does not know the facade.
- Service layer in application architecture is often a facade.

## Pitfalls
- God object: a facade that accumulates all logic and ends up violating SRP. Keep it thin; split into several facades per use case area.
- Hidden failure modes: a "simple" call that triggers five remote calls needs timeouts, idempotency and documented partial-failure behaviour.
- Don't force every client through it if they need fine control.

patterns-1:6.body:
## Problem
You want to control access to an object (lazy creation, access checks, caching, remote call, logging) without clients knowing. A Proxy has the same interface as the real subject and sits in front of it, deciding if and when to forward the call.

## Types
- Virtual proxy: delay an expensive creation until first use (lazy load of a big file or DB connection).
- Protection proxy: check permissions before forwarding.
- Caching proxy: memoise results.
- Remote proxy: local stand-in for a remote service (RPC stubs, gRPC clients, ORM lazy relations).
- Logging/smart-reference proxy: counting, locking, reference management.

## Example
```ts
interface Images { load(id: string): Promise<Buffer> }
class S3Images implements Images { async load(id: string) { /* network */ return Buffer.alloc(0); } }

class CachingImages implements Images {
  private cache = new Map<string, Buffer>();
  constructor(private real: Images) {}
  async load(id: string) {
    const hit = this.cache.get(id);
    if (hit) return hit;
    const buf = await this.real.load(id);
    this.cache.set(id, buf);
    return buf;
  }
}
```

## JavaScript has a built-in Proxy
```ts
const guarded = new Proxy(settings, {
  get: (t, k, r) => Reflect.get(t, k, r),
  set: (t, k, v) => { if (k === "port" && typeof v !== "number") throw new TypeError(); return Reflect.set(t, k, v); },
});
```
Used for validation, observable state (Vue 3 reactivity), default values and API mocking. Costs: slower property access than a plain object (traps), and `===` identity differs from the target; private `#fields` do not work through a Proxy because the method receives the proxy as `this`.

## Proxy vs Decorator vs Adapter
Same interface in Proxy and Decorator; Proxy manages the lifecycle of the subject and controls access, Decorator is stacked by the client to enrich. Adapter changes the interface.

## Pitfalls
- Added latency; cache proxy brings staleness and invalidation concerns.
- A remote proxy hides network failure: callers treat it as local. Add timeouts and error handling; "RPC looks like a function call" is a known trap.
- Reverse proxies (nginx) are the same idea at the network level: load balance, TLS, caching.

patterns-1:7.body:
## Problem
Your model is a tree: files and folders, UI components containing components, menu items, an organisation chart, a price of a bundle containing items and sub-bundles. Client code shouldn't have to distinguish a single item from a group. Composite lets leaves and containers share one interface so they can be treated uniformly, and a container's operation recurses over its children.

## Example: file system size
```ts
interface Node { name: string; size(): number }

class File implements Node {
  constructor(readonly name: string, private bytes: number) {}
  size() { return this.bytes; }
}
class Folder implements Node {
  private children: Node[] = [];
  constructor(readonly name: string) {}
  add(n: Node) { this.children.push(n); return this; }
  size() { return this.children.reduce((s, c) => s + c.size(), 0); }   // recursion
}

const root = new Folder("root").add(new File("a.txt", 100)).add(new Folder("src").add(new File("b.ts", 250)));
root.size();   // 350
```

## Design choices
- Where to put child management (`add`, `remove`)? Transparent: on the shared interface (uniform but leaves must throw or no-op, an ISP smell). Safe: only on the composite (type-safe, but clients must know which is which). Most TS code picks safe.
- Parent pointers help upward traversal, but complicate removal and cycles.
- Caching aggregate values (like size) needs invalidation on child change.

## Where it shows up
DOM and React component trees, AST nodes, permission groups containing roles, UI toolkits, JSON-like structures, shopping bundles, expression trees (`Add(Num, Mul(...))` evaluated recursively), graphics scene graphs.

## Pitfalls
- Deep trees: recursion depth limits, so use an explicit stack or iterator for very deep structures (a 100,000-deep chain will overflow).
- Cycles (a folder that contains itself) cause infinite recursion; validate on add.
- An overly general component interface forces meaningless operations on leaves.

## Related
Often combined with Iterator (traverse), Visitor (operate on all node types), and Decorator (similar recursive structure but one child).

patterns-2:0.body:
## Problem
A class has several interchangeable ways of doing one job (sorting, pricing, routing, compression, auth). Embedding them with `if/else` makes it grow and forces edits for each new variant. Strategy extracts each algorithm into its own object behind a common interface; the context holds one and delegates. The algorithm can be swapped at runtime.

## Example
```ts
interface ShippingStrategy { cost(weightKg: number): number }
const standard: ShippingStrategy = { cost: w => 5 + w * 0.5 };
const express: ShippingStrategy = { cost: w => 15 + w * 1.2 };

class Cart {
  constructor(private shipping: ShippingStrategy) {}
  setShipping(s: ShippingStrategy) { this.shipping = s; }
  total(items: number, kg: number) { return items + this.shipping.cost(kg); }
}
```
In JS/TS a strategy can simply be a function: `Array.sort(compareFn)` is Strategy, and so is `passport.use(new LocalStrategy(...))`. Name the pattern, then show it as a function to prove you know the idiom.

## Choosing at runtime
Registry: `const strategies = { standard, express }; strategies[req.body.method]`. Selection logic can live in a factory so the context stays unaware.

## Strategy vs similar
- State: also swaps a delegate, but the states change themselves based on transitions and know each other; strategies are chosen externally and are independent.
- Template Method: varies steps via inheritance (compile-time); Strategy varies the whole algorithm via composition (runtime).
- Command: encapsulates a request/action; Strategy encapsulates a how.

## Pros and cons
- Pros: Open/Closed, eliminates conditionals, each algorithm unit-testable, swap at runtime.
- Cons: more objects; clients must know the differences to pick one; overkill for two stable variants (a simple conditional is clearer).

## Pitfalls
Strategies that need different inputs push you towards fat interfaces or a context passing everything; pass a small parameter object instead. Stateful strategies shared across requests may have concurrency issues in Node if they hold per-request data.

patterns-2:1.body:
## Problem
When one object changes, an unknown number of others must react (UI updates, cache invalidation, notifications), but the object shouldn't be hard-wired to them. Observer defines a one-to-many dependency: a subject keeps a list of observers and notifies them on change. Publishers and subscribers know only the small interface between them.

## Node's EventEmitter
```ts
import { EventEmitter } from "node:events";

class OrderService extends EventEmitter {
  place(order: { id: string }) {
    /* persist */
    this.emit("placed", order);          // synchronous call of each listener, in registration order
  }
}
const svc = new OrderService();
svc.on("placed", o => console.log("email", o.id));
svc.on("placed", o => console.log("analytics", o.id));
svc.once("shipped", () => {});
const off = (o: unknown) => {};
svc.off("placed", off);                  // remove listener to avoid leaks
```

## Facts worth knowing
- `emit` calls listeners synchronously, in order; the order of `on` calls matters, and a slow listener blocks the emitter. For async work, queue it.
- An exception thrown in a listener propagates to `emit`'s caller (unless caught).
- An `"error"` event with no listener throws and can crash the process; always attach one.
- Default max listeners is 10 per event; exceeding prints a MaxListenersExceededWarning that is a leak hint (listeners added per request and never removed).
- `on` vs `once`; `prependListener`; `removeAllListeners`. Async listeners return promises that `emit` ignores: rejections become unhandled rejections.
- Streams, HTTP servers, child processes and the DOM are all emitters.

## Hand-rolled typed version
```ts
type Listener<T> = (v: T) => void;
class Subject<T> {
  private ls = new Set<Listener<T>>();
  subscribe(l: Listener<T>) { this.ls.add(l); return () => this.ls.delete(l); }   // unsubscribe fn
  next(v: T) { for (const l of [...this.ls]) l(v); }   // copy: safe if a listener unsubscribes
}
```

## Observer vs Pub/Sub
Observer: subject holds direct references to observers, in-process, usually synchronous. Pub/sub: a broker (Redis, Kafka, SNS) sits between, with topics, decoupled in time and space, async, can cross processes. EventEmitter is in-process observer, not a durable message bus: events are lost on a crash and don't cross cluster workers.

## Pitfalls
Memory leaks from forgotten unsubscribes; surprising cascades (A emits, B's listener emits, ...); ordering assumptions; hard-to-trace control flow. RxJS Observables add operators, completion and cancellation.

patterns-2:2.body:
## Problem
You want to treat a request as an object: queue it, log it, retry it, undo it, send it over the wire, or let a button trigger it without knowing what it does. Command wraps an action (receiver plus method plus arguments) in an object with a uniform `execute()`.

## Example: undo stack in an editor
```ts
interface Command { execute(): void; undo(): void }

class InsertText implements Command {
  constructor(private doc: Doc, private pos: number, private text: string) {}
  execute() { this.doc.insert(this.pos, this.text); }
  undo()    { this.doc.remove(this.pos, this.text.length); }
}

class History {
  private done: Command[] = [];
  private undone: Command[] = [];
  run(c: Command) { c.execute(); this.done.push(c); this.undone = []; }
  undo() { const c = this.done.pop(); if (c) { c.undo(); this.undone.push(c); } }
  redo() { const c = this.undone.pop(); if (c) { c.execute(); this.done.push(c); } }
}
```
Roles: Client builds commands, Invoker (History/button) runs them, Receiver (Doc) does the real work.

## Where it appears in backend work
- Job queues (BullMQ, SQS): a job is a serialised command with a handler.
- Event sourcing: commands validated, then produce events; CQRS command side.
- Transactional outbox / saga steps, each with a compensating action (the undo).
- Task schedulers, macro recorders, CLI subcommands, Redux actions (plain-object commands handled by reducers).
- In JS a simple form is a closure: `() => doc.insert(...)`; add undo only if needed.

## Benefits
Decouples invoker from receiver; supports queueing, scheduling, logging and audit; undo/redo; composite commands (macro) combine several.

## Costs and pitfalls
- Class per action = boilerplate; closures or data plus handler map lower it.
- Undo needs captured state (the previous value) or inverse operations; side-effects such as emails cannot be undone, only compensated.
- Commands in a queue must be idempotent or deduplicated, since at-least-once delivery can run them twice.
- Memory growth of history; cap it.

## Vs Strategy
Strategy: choose how to do one thing; Command: reify a thing to be done, with its parameters, at a time of your choosing.

patterns-2:3.body:
## Problem
An object behaves differently depending on its current state (order: pending, paid, shipped, cancelled; TCP connection: closed, listen, established), and the code ends up with `switch(this.state)` in every method, with illegal transitions slipping through. State encapsulates each state's behaviour in its own object; the context delegates to the current state, and states trigger transitions.

## Example: order lifecycle
```ts
interface OrderState {
  pay(o: Order): void; ship(o: Order): void; cancel(o: Order): void;
}
class Pending implements OrderState {
  pay(o: Order) { o.setState(new Paid()); }
  ship() { throw new Error("pay first"); }
  cancel(o: Order) { o.setState(new Cancelled()); }
}
class Paid implements OrderState {
  pay() { throw new Error("already paid"); }
  ship(o: Order) { o.setState(new Shipped()); }
  cancel(o: Order) { o.setState(new Cancelled()); /* plus refund */ }
}
class Shipped implements OrderState { pay(){throw new Error()} ship(){throw new Error()} cancel(){throw new Error("too late")} }
class Cancelled implements OrderState { pay(){throw new Error()} ship(){throw new Error()} cancel(){} }

class Order {
  private state: OrderState = new Pending();
  setState(s: OrderState) { this.state = s; }
  pay() { this.state.pay(this); } ship() { this.state.ship(this); } cancel() { this.state.cancel(this); }
}
```

## Lightweight alternative: transition table
For simple machines, data beats classes:
```ts
type S = "pending" | "paid" | "shipped" | "cancelled";
const next: Record<S, Partial<Record<"pay"|"ship"|"cancel", S>>> = {
  pending: { pay: "paid", cancel: "cancelled" },
  paid: { ship: "shipped", cancel: "cancelled" },
  shipped: {}, cancelled: {},
};
function apply(s: S, ev: "pay"|"ship"|"cancel"): S { const n = next[s][ev]; if (!n) throw new Error("illegal"); return n; }
```
Libraries: XState for complex statecharts. For persisted entities, store the state in the DB and enforce transitions with a conditional update (`WHERE status='pending'`) to be safe under concurrency.

## State vs Strategy
Structurally identical. State: the object changes its own delegate, states know about each other, behaviour depends on history. Strategy: client picks it, strategies independent.

## Pitfalls
Many classes for few states; shared data must live in the context; states holding per-instance data cannot be singletons. Add a new state means touching transitions in neighbours.

patterns-2:4.body:
## Problem
A request must pass through several processing steps (authentication, rate limiting, validation, logging, handler), and the set or order varies by route. Hard-wiring them couples sender to every receiver. Chain of Responsibility links handlers: each one either handles the request, passes it to the next, or short-circuits the chain.

## Express middleware is the pattern
```ts
import express, { Request, Response, NextFunction } from "express";
const app = express();

app.use((req, _res, next) => { console.log(req.method, req.url); next(); });       // logging
const auth = (req: Request, res: Response, next: NextFunction) => {
  if (!req.headers.authorization) return res.status(401).end();                       // short-circuit
  next();
};
app.get("/admin", auth, (_req, res) => res.send("secret"));

app.use((err: Error, _req: Request, res: Response, _next: NextFunction) => {          // 4 args = error handler
  res.status(500).json({ error: "internal" });
});
```

## How it works
- Each middleware receives (req, res, next). Calling `next()` passes control to the next in registration order; sending a response without calling `next()` ends the chain.
- `next(err)` skips to the next error-handling middleware (4 parameters).
- Order is semantics: body parser before routes, auth before handlers, error handler last, CORS early.
- Async: Express 4 does not catch rejected promises in async handlers; use try/catch with `next(e)` or a wrapper. Express 5 forwards rejections to the error handler. Koa composes middleware as nested async functions with `await next()`, so code after it runs on the way back (onion model).

## Hand-rolled chain
```ts
type Handler = (req: Req, next: () => Promise<Res>) => Promise<Res>;
const compose = (hs: Handler[]) => (req: Req) =>
  hs.reduceRight<(r: Req) => Promise<Res>>((n, h) => r => h(r, () => n(r)), async () => ({ status: 404 }))(req);
```

## Same idea elsewhere
Servlet filters, ASP.NET pipeline, axios interceptors, event bubbling in the DOM, support escalation tiers, approval workflows (manager, director, VP).

## Pitfalls
- A request may fall off the end unhandled; add a terminal 404 handler.
- Forgetting `next()` hangs the request until timeout; calling it after sending a response causes "headers already sent".
- Long chains cost latency per request and are hard to debug; log which handler ended the chain.
- Versus Decorator: decorators always delegate; chain members may stop it.

patterns-2:5.body:
## Problem
Several procedures share the same skeleton but differ in a few steps (import CSV vs JSON then validate then save; build a report; run a test: setup, run, teardown). Copying the skeleton duplicates the flow and its bugs. Template Method defines the algorithm's skeleton in a base class method and lets subclasses override specific steps, without changing the order.

## Example
```ts
abstract class DataImporter {
  // the template: not meant to be overridden
  async run(path: string) {
    const raw = await this.read(path);
    const rows = this.parse(raw);
    const valid = rows.filter(r => this.validate(r));
    await this.save(valid);
    this.afterImport(valid.length);        // hook with default behaviour
  }
  protected abstract parse(raw: string): Row[];            // required step
  protected validate(r: Row): boolean { return true; }     // optional override
  protected afterImport(_n: number) {}                     // hook
  private async read(p: string) { return (await import("node:fs/promises")).readFile(p, "utf8"); }
  private async save(rows: Row[]) { /* bulk insert */ }
}
class CsvImporter extends DataImporter { protected parse(raw: string): Row[] { /* ... */ return []; } }
```
The Hollywood principle: "don't call us, we'll call you": the base class calls the subclass code.

## Step kinds
- Abstract steps: must implement.
- Hooks: optional empty/default methods.
- Fixed steps: private or `final` in Java (`final void run()`) so subclasses cannot reorder them. TS has no `final` for methods; use convention or `private`.

## Template Method vs Strategy
- Template: inheritance, variation of steps, chosen at compile time, one hierarchy; subclass is coupled to base.
- Strategy: composition, swap the whole algorithm at runtime. Prefer Strategy (or pass step functions into a function) if you want flexibility or the base class would need many hooks.
- Functional equivalent in JS: a function taking callbacks: `runImport({ parse, validate })`.

## Real examples
Framework lifecycle hooks (React class `componentDidMount`, JUnit `setUp/tearDown`, Spring's JdbcTemplate callbacks), Node stream `_transform` / `_write` implementations, test runners, Java's `AbstractList`.

## Pitfalls
Fragile base class; Liskov violations when a subclass breaks the skeleton's assumptions; too many hooks make the flow hard to follow; calling overridable methods from a base constructor runs before the subclass fields are initialised (a Java/C++/TS gotcha; in C++ it calls the base version, not the derived one).

patterns-2:6.body:
## Problem
You want to walk through a collection (list, tree, paginated API, DB cursor) without exposing how it is stored, and possibly have multiple independent traversals at once. Iterator moves traversal into a separate object with `next()` semantics.

## JavaScript has it built in
Iteration protocol: an object is iterable if it has a `[Symbol.iterator]()` method returning an iterator, an object with `next()` returning `{ value, done }`. `for...of`, spread, destructuring and `Array.from` all use it.
```ts
class Range implements Iterable<number> {
  constructor(private from: number, private to: number) {}
  *[Symbol.iterator]() { for (let i = this.from; i <= this.to; i++) yield i; }   // generator
}
console.log([...new Range(1, 5)]);   // [1,2,3,4,5]
```
Generators (`function*` with `yield`) are the easy way to write iterators; they are lazy, so values are computed on demand and infinite sequences are possible.

## Tree traversal without exposing the structure
```ts
class TreeNode<T> {
  constructor(public value: T, public children: TreeNode<T>[] = []) {}
  *[Symbol.iterator](): Generator<T> { yield this.value; for (const c of this.children) yield* c; }
}
```

## Async iterators
```ts
async function* pages(url: string) {
  let next: string | undefined = url;
  while (next) { const r = await (await fetch(next)).json(); yield* r.items; next = r.nextPage; }
}
for await (const item of pages("/api/items")) { /* process lazily, bounded memory */ }
```
The same mechanism backs Node streams (`for await (const chunk of stream)`) and MongoDB cursors. Benefit: memory stays O(page) even for millions of rows.

## Facts and pitfalls
- Iterators are single-use; an exhausted one stays done. An iterable creates a fresh iterator each time.
- Modifying a collection while iterating can skip or repeat elements, or throw (Java ConcurrentModificationException; fail-fast iterators).
- `for...in` iterates keys (including inherited), not values: not the iterator protocol.
- `return()` is called on early `break` so iterators can release resources (close the cursor).
- Java: `Iterable<T>` and `Iterator<T>` (`hasNext/next/remove`); C++: STL iterators (begin/end, categories such as random access).
- External iterator (client calls next) vs internal iterator (`forEach(cb)`).

patterns-2:7.body:
## Problem
When many components talk directly to each other, you get an N-by-N web of dependencies (up to N(N-1)/2 links), changes ripple, and nothing is reusable alone. Mediator centralises the interaction: components know only the mediator and send it events; it coordinates and routes to the others. Many-to-many becomes many-to-one.

## Example: chat room
```ts
interface Mediator { send(from: User, msg: string): void }

class ChatRoom implements Mediator {
  private users = new Set<User>();
  join(u: User) { this.users.add(u); }
  send(from: User, msg: string) {
    for (const u of this.users) if (u !== from) u.receive(from.name, msg);
  }
}
class User {
  constructor(readonly name: string, private room: Mediator) {}
  say(m: string) { this.room.send(this, m); }
  receive(from: string, m: string) { console.log(this.name + " got " + m + " from " + from); }
}
```
Users never reference each other. Adding moderation, rate limits or logging changes only the room.

## Real examples
- UI dialogs: a form controller enabling/disabling widgets based on others' state.
- Air traffic control tower; message brokers (broader, async variant); Redux store or event bus coordinating components; MediatR in .NET; a CQRS command bus dispatching to handlers.
- Express app object or an orchestrator service coordinating a workflow (orchestration vs choreography in microservices: a saga orchestrator is a mediator).

## Mediator vs neighbours
- Observer: one-way broadcast, subjects don't know who listens; Mediator: two-way coordination logic sits in one place and components do know it. Mediator is often implemented using Observer.
- Facade: simplifies a subsystem's interface for clients, subsystem objects unaware; Mediator: colleagues are aware and communicate through it.

## Trade-offs
- Pros: fewer dependencies, easier to understand interactions in one place, components reusable.
- Cons: the mediator can swell into a god object and single point of complexity (SRP violation); orchestration through a central service becomes a bottleneck and single point of failure in distributed setups (choreography with events avoids that at the cost of traceability).

## Pitfalls
Put coordination rules only in the mediator and keep components dumb; split into several mediators per workflow; avoid cyclic event storms where the mediator notifies a component, which sends another event back.
